    "description": "Paper recycling recovers fibres from used paper and cardboard for new products. Historical research from Isfahan illustrates the importance of collection and separation, while technical sources explain why recovered paper still requires cleaning and processing.",
    "tags": ["Paper recycling", "Cardboard", "Recovered fibre", "Isfahan", "Waste separation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T06:43:25Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:43:25Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Paper Recycling in Iran: Collection, Fibre and Evidence",
      "description": "Understand paper recycling through historical Isfahan research, the recovered fibre process, and the limits of available national evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "recovering-fibre",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recovering fibre rather than preserving the sheet",
        "content": "Paper recycling breaks down used sheets and packaging so their fibres can become raw material again. At a recycled paper mill, recovered paper is mixed with water in a pulper, which separates the fibres. Screens and other cleaning equipment then remove unwanted material. Depending on the intended product, additional treatment may remove ink. The result is a fibre suspension that can be formed into new paper. [s1]\n\nThis process explains why paper collected for recycling is not equivalent to finished recycled paper. Incoming material still needs processing, and its suitability depends partly on contamination and the product being made. The United States Environmental Protection Agency's technical explanation describes these general principles; it does not establish which equipment a particular Iranian mill operates. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "isfahan-case",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A historical view from Isfahan",
        "content": "A study of Isfahan, a major city in central Iran, examined paper and paperboard recycling using field observations, archived records and an interview with the recycling organization. Its account of the study year, 2006, described much of the material being discarded after mixing with wet municipal waste. The authors concluded that recycling had economic and environmental justification. [s2]\n\nThis is evidence about a particular place and reporting period. It cannot establish today's national recycling performance. Its continuing explanatory value lies in the collection problem it documents: material with potential value can leave the recovery chain before it reaches a mill. The study's environmental savings were reported estimates, rather than direct measurements of every avoided impact, and are not reproduced here as universal conversion factors. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "collection-and-quality",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Collection and processing are separate stages",
        "content": "The American Forest & Paper Association describes a collection chain in which paper is separated from other recyclables, contaminants are removed, and the material is baled for transport to a mill. At the mill, pulping and screening prepare the fibres for new products. This is a useful process model for understanding the distinction between collection infrastructure and manufacturing capacity. It is not a map of an Iranian municipal service. [s3]\n\nThe association also emphasizes checking what a local collection system accepts. A material's theoretical recyclability does not establish that a particular collector or mill will handle it. For an account of Iran, verified local acceptance rules and actual downstream destinations would therefore be more informative than a generic list of everything made from paper. Those details remain unverified in this draft. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "limits-and-outlook",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A useful loop with material limits",
        "content": "Paper fibres cannot circulate indefinitely: repeated processing shortens them, and fibre characteristics affect which new products they can make. Mills may use recovered fibre alone or blend it with virgin fibre. Recycling consequently belongs within a broader material system, rather than representing a permanent closed loop. [s1]\n\nFor Iran, the evidence assembled here supports an explanation of the process and a historical collection challenge. A current assessment needs comparable measurements of paper generated, collected, accepted by mills and converted into products. Keeping those stages distinct would make future claims about progress substantially easier to evaluate. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
