    "description": "Electronic waste management addresses discarded electrical and electronic equipment, combining material recovery with pollution prevention. Research on Tehran identifies governance and infrastructure priorities, while international health evidence explains why recycling methods matter.",
    "tags": ["Electronic waste", "Tehran", "Recycling", "Environmental health", "Waste governance"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T06:43:25Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:43:25Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Electronic Waste Management in Iran: Tehran and Safe Recovery",
      "description": "Explore Iran's electronic waste challenge through Tehran research, material recovery risks, and the distinction between policy priorities and results."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "equipment-and-hazards",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A waste stream with value and hazards",
        "content": "Electronic waste, often shortened to e-waste, consists of discarded electrical and electronic equipment. It includes familiar consumer devices as well as larger appliances and medical equipment. The World Health Organization emphasizes that these products contain recoverable resources, but that inappropriate processing can release hazardous substances. Material value and environmental risk therefore coexist within the same waste stream. [s1]\n\nThe condition and handling of equipment matter. WHO distinguishes ordinary use of functional products from environmentally unsound practices after disposal. Burning, heating and uncontrolled chemical processing can contaminate air, soil, dust and water. Children and pregnant women are particularly vulnerable to hazardous exposure. These are international health findings; they do not establish the prevalence of particular practices or health outcomes in Iran. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tehran-research",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What Tehran research identifies",
        "content": "A study published in Scientific Reports in February 2026 examined electronic waste management in Tehran through a structured assessment involving municipal waste officials and private recycling managers. The researchers combined an analysis of strengths, weaknesses, opportunities and threats with a method for ranking proposed strategies. Its purpose was to identify management priorities, rather than to measure a national recycling rate. [s2]\n\nThe study identified gaps in supervision, infrastructure and technical expertise. Its highest-ranked priorities included comprehensive health, safety and environmental rules for recycling, improvements to collection, and dedicated sorting facilities. These findings help explain why a recovery system requires more than equipment for extracting valuable materials. Coordination, oversight and collection arrangements also shape how material reaches processors. [s2]\n\nThe results need careful interpretation. They reflect a stakeholder-based strategic assessment of the capital. A highly ranked proposal is not evidence that a facility has been built, a rule has been enforced or pollution has fallen. Nor should priorities identified in Tehran automatically be treated as representative of all Iranian cities. The distinction between a proposed intervention and its demonstrated outcome is central to reading this research. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluating-progress",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Following the chain beyond collection",
        "content": "Taken together, the Tehran research and WHO guidance suggest a useful way to evaluate progress: follow discarded equipment through collection and sorting to its eventual treatment. A collection initiative establishes little about final environmental performance unless the subsequent handling is also documented. This is an editorial inference from the management gaps identified in Tehran and the processing hazards described by WHO. [s1][s2]\n\nThe same caution applies to the word recycling. Recovering saleable material can coexist with unsafe treatment of other components. Evidence about recovery should therefore be considered alongside evidence about pollution prevention and worker protection. [s1]\n\nFor an international reader, Iran's electronic waste challenge is consequently both an industrial and a governance subject. The inspected sources support that framing, but do not establish current nationwide collection coverage, treatment capacity or compliance. Those questions require additional Iranian operating records and independently assessed outcomes. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
